  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the evolution of dye-sensitized and perovskite solar-cell designs over time and links these device architectures to the molecular sensitizers used in them. A timeline across the top shows the progression from early TiO2 dye sensitization and the first liquid-electrolyte DSSCs, to solid-state DSSCs, perovskite-sensitized solar cells, the first full perovskite solar cells, planar PSCs, and more recent dye-linked perovskite cells. The small device schematics emphasize changes in layer structure: porous or mesoporous TiO2 coated with dye, liquid or solid hole-transport materials, perovskite infiltrating mesoporous films, and later compact planar stacks with electron- and hole-transport layers around a perovskite absorber.\n\nThe lower part compares two families of sensitizers. Metal-centered dyes are illustrated with a central metal, anchoring ligands, and ancillary ligands, followed by examples for n-type and p-type metal-oxide sensitization. Below that, metal-free dyes are shown as donor-linker-acceptor molecules, again with examples for n-type and p-type sensitization. Overall, the figure conveys how solar-cell development moved from dye-driven interfacial charge transfer toward perovskite absorber devices, while still drawing on sensitizer design concepts such as anchoring groups, donor–acceptor structures, and transport-layer matching.",
